## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical end-to-end config provenance and policy enforcement for multi-tenant CNF platforms, a common production risk area beyond image provenance.

## Key Concepts
- Config payload signing with Cosign and KMS-backed keys
- SBOM attachment for config changes
- Admission control with Kyverno/OPA enforcing signature, SBOM, and tenant labels
- Canary namespace validation before rollout
- Per-tenant rollback mechanisms and status surfacing

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo workflow: verify config update integrity before applying
async function verifyConfigUpdate(payload, signature, sbom) {
  const okSig = await verifySignature(payload, signature);
  const okSBOM = await validateSBOM(payload, sbom);
  const tenantOk = checkTenantLabel(payload);
  return okSig && okSBOM && tenantOk;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test rollback semantics across tenants?
- How would you handle SBOM drift when multiple tenants update concurrently?

## Why This Question Is Asked
This question evaluates understanding of multi-tenant mTLS, dynamic certificate reloading, and sidecar patterns. It tests knowledge of secure-by-default design and how certificate rotation integrates with live production traffic.

## Key Concepts
- Per-tenant TLS materials and certificate management
- Sidecar dynamic TLS reload mechanisms
- Internal CA API integration and certificate rotation
- Header-based tenant identification (X-TENANT-ID)
- Gateway validation rules and trust models

## Code Example
```go
// Fetch tenant certificate, cache with TTL, and wire into TLS config
func (s *Sidecar) getClientCert(tenantID string) (*tls.Certificate, error) {
    if cert, ok := s.certCache.Get(tenantID); ok {
        return cert, nil
    }
    cert, err := s.caClient.FetchCertificate(tenantID)
    if err != nil {
        return nil, err
    }
    s.certCache.Set(tenantID, cert, certTTL)
    return cert, nil
}
```
